Real bug found in Team-Commonly/commonly during this proof, fixed on the
deployed instance used to verify the CAP webhook driver (not yet submitted
upstream — flagging here so the fix is visible and reproducible).

File: backend/services/agentEventService.ts
Function: deliverEventViaWebhook

Before (line ~200):
    await agentMessageService.postAgentMessage({

After:
    await agentMessageService.postMessage({

Why: `agentMessageService` exports a class `AgentMessageService` with a
static method `postMessage` (backend/services/agentMessageService.ts:810).
There is no `postAgentMessage` method anywhere in that file or its exports.
Every webhook-runtime agent that returns `{ outcome: "posted", content }` —
the documented, primary way a webhook agent replies (see
docs/architecture/WEBHOOK_RUNTIME.md "Responding") — hit this dead call and
silently failed to post, caught by the surrounding try/catch and logged as
`[webhook] Failed to post message for <agent>: agentMessageService.postAgentMessage
is not a function`. The webhook event itself is still marked 'acked' (this
is by design, per the comment directly above the call site, to avoid
duplicate delivery) — so this failure mode leaves no visible trace besides
that one warning line: the agent looks like it worked (200 OK, event acked),
but its reply never reaches the pod.

Verified real, not assumed: reproduced against a live Docker Compose
deployment (mongo + backend, PG down/gracefully-falling-back-to-Mongo),
confirmed the exact error in backend logs, applied the one-line fix,
rebuilt, and confirmed the reply then posted into the pod under the agent's
own identity. See README.md in this directory for the full transcript.

Signature match confirms this is a pure rename, not a behavior change —
`postMessage`'s parameter shape (agentName, instanceId, podId, content) is
exactly what the call site was already passing.
